        {
          "code": "CONFOUNDING",
          "text": "Game context (score, period, lineup, opponent defense, substitution patterns, transition vs half-court) strongly affects both the probability of a drive and the efficiency of a subsequent PNR."
        }
      ]
    },
    "prior_work": {
      "already_answered": "partly",
      "cited": [
        "FAM:SF-APPA-058",
        "FAM:SF-CHT-025",
        "FAM:SF-APPS-011",
        "FAM:SF-APPA-050"
      ],
      "summary": "Prior project research has directly examined one DRIVE→PNR teammate pair and found a negative premium (Giannis→Lillard, -0.263, n=31). It also discovered systematic foul leakage in DRIVE labelling and established that play-type PPP is context-dependent. No prior study has attempted the general hypothesis that any teammate DRIVE raises PNR PPP, but the existing evidence collectively indicates substantial measurement and inference barriers."
    },
    "risks": [
      {
        "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
        "text": "DRIVE labels are contaminated by foul leakage: an unknown fraction of true drives are missed or misclassified, and the criteria for labelling a possession as DRIVE may correlate with its outcome, biasing any sequence comparison."
      },
      {
        "code": "CONFOUNDING",
        "text": "Numerous game-state and lineup confounders (score margin, remaining time, possession shot clock, defensive scheme, rest days) could produce spurious associations between a preceding drive and PNR efficiency."
      },
      {
        "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
        "text": "The combination of requiring a teammate DRIVE and a following PNR by the same team may yield sparse cell counts, as seen in SF-APPA-058, making estimates noisy and sensitive to outliers."
      },
      {
        "code": "LEAKAGE_LOOKAHEAD",
        "text": "If play-type labels are derived using post-hoc classification that considers the outcome of the possession (e.g., points scored), the label 'DRIVE' may be applied differently to successful and unsuccessful possessions, introducing look-ahead bias."
      },
      {
        "code": "MULTIPLE_TESTING",
        "text": "Testing across many possible pairings or possession intervals would require pre-specified adjustment to avoid inflated false-positive rates."
      },
      {
        "code": "DRIFT_NONSTATIONARITY",
        "text": "Playing style and play-type definitions may evolve across seasons, threatening the stability of any estimated effect when pooling 2022-23 through 2025-26 data."
      }
    ],
    "predicted_outcome": {
      "supported": 0.1,
      "rejected": 0.2,
      "untestable": 0.3,
      "artifact": 0.3,
      "uncertain": 0.1
    },
    "decision": "do_not_proceed",
    "design": {
      "estimand": "The average difference in PNR PPP (points scored directly from the PNR action divided by the number of PNR possessions) when the immediately preceding team possession was a DRIVE, minus the average PNR PPP when the preceding possession was any other non-DRIVE action, within the same lineup stint and controlling for game context.",
      "unit": "A team possession (offensive trip down the floor until a shot, turnover, or foul ends the possession).",
      "sample": "All regular-season and playoff possessions from 2022-23 through 2025-26 where the offensive team runs a PNR, excluding overtime and possessions with a preceding time-out or inter-period gap, and retaining only possessions where the preceding possession can be unambiguously linked to the same team and same stint.",
      "power": "Cannot be assessed without a reliable drive label. Even with large N, measurement error in DRIVE will attenuate effect sizes and reduce effective power. A formal power analysis would require an effect size estimate from validated labels; the single available estimate is -0.263 with n=31, suggesting any positive effect is small at best.",
      "controls": "Game period and score margin at start of possession, shot clock at start of possession (if available), home/away, starting vs bench lineup indicator, opponent defensive rating percentile, and an interaction with regular season/playoffs.",
      "falsification": "1) Test for balance on observable pre-treatment characteristics (e.g., shot clock, score margin, opponent defensive strength) between drive-preceded and non-drive-preceded PNR possessions. 2) Verify that the sequence labelling (preceding possession is a drive by any teammate) does not predict game outcomes or other future events beyond the immediate PNR, implying no look-ahead leakage. 3) Reproduce the known foul leakage pattern for DRIVE possessions in the analysis sample to confirm the measurement problem is present.",
      "multiplicity": "A single pre-registered hypothesis test on the pooled sample, with a pre-specified subgroup analysis by team if sample permits, using Holm-Bonferroni correction for the number of team subgroups tested.",
      "decision_rule": "Reject the null at α=0.05 if the adjusted p-value for the ATE of teammate DRIVE on PNR PPP is <0.05 AND the falsification checks pass. If the falsification checks reveal substantial imbalance or label leakage, the result will be considered invalid regardless of p-value.",
      "data_version": "2022-23 through 2025-26 seasons from per-game play-by-play, with the graph-derived play-type possession records. However, this design is not executable with current data because SF-CHT-025 demonstrated that DRIVE labels are not sufficiently reliable. The minimum viable version would require externally validated or corrected drive labels (e.g., from manual annotation or tracking data) before the sequence analysis can be trusted."
    }
  }
}

